{"ts": "2026-11-09T02:42:44.962569Z", "run_id": "r-20261109T0242Z-c584", "unit": "openspec:add-fake-egress-proxy", "seq": 4, "type": "pr.labels", "number": 106, "labels": ["l4:verifying"]}
{"ts": "2026-11-09T02:42:46.378179Z", "run_id": "r-20261109T0242Z-c584", "unit": "openspec:add-fake-egress-proxy", "seq": 5, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-004", "target": "F-39583b05", "decision": "reject", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding describes the code accurately and the behaviour it names is a defect of the record, not a harmless detail. In the proxy module the unreachable path calls req.resume() and then finish(502) at once, and finish takes the digest from hash.copy(), so for a connection error the recorded bodySha256 covers only the request bytes that had already flowed before the upstream socket failed; a large or chunked body gives a timing-dependent digest. The no-route path is worse: it awaits readAll(req), which resolves the hex digest string, and feeds that string into the hash, so the record holds the SHA-256 of a hex digest rather than of the body, even for an empty body. The design says the body streams to the upstream while its SHA-256 is computed, that refusals read the body to its end before recording, and that the unreachable case is recorded with status 502 and outcome error, so the record's bodySha256 is meant to be the digest of the whole body in that case too. No scenario pins it only because the pass-through rows send GET without a body and the test compares path, status, outcome and host, so the fix is checked by the sentence below. WHEN a request with a body is forwarded to an upstream the proxy cannot reach, whether because the target host has no route and is not loopback or because the connection fails before response headers, THEN the proxy reads the request body to its end before it appends the ForwardedRequest and the record's bodySha256 equals the hex SHA-256 of the whole request body.", "text_sha256": "4c58b25969bccef74ed99b26c3455b57219e6305c7d0d493217c9266bb43180a", "answer_sha256": "ed988c2c75065486eef30b5de6c7b2ef742514087a5e9cd5bb62ba6361b105bd", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-11-09T02:42:46.514640Z", "run_id": "r-20261109T0242Z-c584", "unit": "openspec:add-fake-egress-proxy", "seq": 6, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-005", "target": "F-e6c2caec", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The fact of the finding is right: no file named proxy-pass-through.test.ts exists under the fake's directory, and the tests for the scenarios Streaming, redirects and upstream errors pass through and The credential-less route is fake-only live in the test file that task 4.1 wrote. That is a deviation in file layout only. Both scenarios are tested with their criterion ids: the gated stream read before any record, the 302 with location, both set-cookie lines and x-up, the 500, the two 502 answers with the exact body, the loopback target, the credential-less route without a credential header and the stored credential winning afterwards, and the four TypeError registrations followed by the 502 vault error. Those tests failed at red under task 4.1 against the minimal proxy, and the production code of task 4.2 is in place, so the task's substance is done and the already-satisfied tick is honest about it. The proposal and design name the modules and ask only that each has tests; neither names the test file. A fix would have to move tests out of a frozen test file, which the project forbids and which only a person could commit, and would change no behaviour. The finding is waived.", "text_sha256": "f17a3217448bc3ed9eee8fd3d6e4eb4e462cd674dcbd21b8fc79b4a9a08fb922", "answer_sha256": "1723c64178cfe22f4823175f5f877afd460060cc24192abf6669134159d1308c", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
